mitter_user":"yossarian","user_is_author":true,"tags":["python"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/kfpoos","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/kfpoos/stricter_checks_for_uploaded"},{"short_id":"4j15up","created_at":"2026-11-18T08:58:05.376-06:00","title":"For the things we can only do together - Bonfire 1.1 Release","url":"https://bonfirenetworks.org/posts/for-the-things-we-can-only-do-together/","score":5,"flags":1,"comment_count":0,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"chobeat","user_is_author":false,"tags":["elixir","release"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/4j15up","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/4j15up/for_things_we_can_only_do_together_bonfire"},{"short_id":"46nwql","created_at":"2026-11-18T12:28:22.187-06:00","title":"Why Fibonacci?","url":"https://www.evilgeniuslabs.org/why-fibonacci","score":3,"flags":0,"comment_count":0,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"fanf","user_is_author":false,"tags":["art","math"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/46nwql","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/46nwql/why_fibonacci"},{"short_id":"yqmfzq","created_at":"2026-11-17T09:23:20.434-06:00","title":"Steve Jobs Core Animation demo from WWDC 2007","url":"https://lucas.love/100/058","score":31,"flags":1,"comment_count":15,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"jeanthomas","user_is_author":false,"tags":["graphics","mac","video"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/yqmfzq","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/yqmfzq/steve_jobs_core_animation_demo_from_wwdc"},{"short_id":"z4rhlt","created_at":"2026-11-17T02:20:30.125-06:00","title":"Redict is deprecated","url":"https://redict.io/posts/2026-11-17-redict-deprecated/","score":74,"flags":0,"comment_count":9,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"val","user_is_author":false,"tags":["databases"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/z4rhlt","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/z4rhlt/redict_is_deprecated"},{"short_id":"3kdl1p","created_at":"2026-11-18T12:13:35.717-06:00","title":"Faster CI with Orchestrated Pytest Workers","url":"https://making.close.com/posts/faster-ci-with-orchestrated-pytest-workers/","score":2,"flags":0,"comment_count":0,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"jonathannen","user_is_author":false,"tags":["python","testing"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/3kdl1p","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/3kdl1p/faster_ci_with_orchestrated_pytest"},{"short_id":"lgdtzb","created_at":"2026-11-18T10:08:03.758-06:00","title":"Context-Generic Programming (CGP) v0.8.0 - cargo-cgp, new documentation, and namespaces","url":"https://contextgeneric.dev/blog/v0.8.0-release","score":1,"flags":0,"comment_count":0,"description":"\u003cp\u003eAuthor here. Context-Generic Programming (CGP) lets a Rust trait have several implementations that would normally overlap, such as one for every \u003ccode\u003eT: Display\u003c/code\u003e and another for every \u003ccode\u003eT: AsRef\u0026lt;[u8]\u0026gt;\u003c/code\u003e. Each application picks the implementation it wants, the compiler resolves that choice to direct calls, and CGP itself is an ordinary library on stable Rust.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eMost of this release is about making CGP easier to learn and debug:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003ecargo-cgp v0.1.0\u003c/strong\u003e explains wiring errors in terms of the code you wrote, rather than the code the macros generated. When a provider needs a field your type lacks, for example, \u003ccode\u003ecargo cgp check\u003c/code\u003e names the missing field and suggests adding it. It leads with the root cause for the classes it recognizes, though it does not yet reshape every class.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eNew documentation\u003c/strong\u003e covers every construct, the core concepts, comparisons with other languages, and real projects. AI agents wrote much of it under my direction, and the post explains how that writing is checked.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eNamespaces\u003c/strong\u003e let an application select whole groups of implementations by path, instead of listing every component in its wiring.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIf you want to try it, the \u003ca href=\"https://contextgeneric.dev/docs/quickstart\" rel=\"ugc\"\u003eQuickstart\u003c/a\u003e is short. And if a diagnostic or an explanation still loses you, I would like to hear where.\u003c/p\u003e\n","description_plain":"Author here. Context-Generic Programming (CGP) lets a Rust trait have several implementations that would normally overlap, such as one for every `T: Display` and another for every `T: AsRef\u003c[u8]\u003e`. Each application picks the implementation it wants, the compiler resolves that choice to direct calls, and CGP itself is an ordinary library on stable Rust.\r\n\r\nMost of this release is about making CGP easier to learn and debug:\r\n\r\n- **cargo-cgp v0.1.0** explains wiring errors in terms of the code you wrote, rather than the code the macros generated. When a provider needs a field your type lacks, for example, `cargo cgp check` names the missing field and suggests adding it. It leads with the root cause for the classes it recognizes, though it does not yet reshape every class.\r\n- **New documentation** covers every construct, the core concepts, comparisons with other languages, and real projects. AI agents wrote much of it under my direction, and the post explains how that writing is checked.\r\n- **Namespaces** let an application select whole groups of implementations by path, instead of listing every component in its wiring.\r\n\r\nIf you want to try it, the [Quickstart](https://contextgeneric.dev/docs/quickstart) is short. And if a diagnostic or an explanation still loses you, I would like to hear where.","submitter_user":"soareschen","user_is_author":true,"tags":["plt","programming","rust"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/lgdtzb","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/lgdtzb/context_generic_programming_cgp_v0_8_0"},{"short_id":"w78jvi","created_at":"2026-11-18T11:35:19.967-06:00","title":"markdownWeb","url":"https://demo.bonbonweb.org/","score":2,"flags":1,"comment_count":0,"description":"","description_plain":"","submitter_user":"matts","user_is_author":false,"tags":["web"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/w78jvi","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/w78jvi/markdownweb"},{"short_id":"kseyz1","created_at":"2026-11-17T03:28:18.950-06:00","title":"Github Redesign (2019)","url":"https://tonsky.me/blog/github-redesign/","score":43,"flags":1,"comment_count":10,"description":"\u003cp\u003eI suggest the conversation steer away from the controversial last point where he flattens the UI quite a bit to make it less \"dated\", as the rest was quite good up until that point, and that deserves being discussed.\u003c/p\u003e\n","description_plain":"I suggest the conversation steer away from the controversial last point where he flattens the UI quite a bit to make it less \"dated\", as the rest was quite good up until that point, and that deserves being discussed.","submitter_user":"decentstates","user_is_author":false,"tags":["design"],"short_id_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/kseyz1","comments_url":"https://lobste.rs/s/kseyz1/github_redesign_2019"}]